---
.title = "Video",
.description = "A native video player over the window, for what the WebView's <video> can't play: camcorder MPEG-TS, WMV, ProRes, or a GPU where the WebView's video is slow.",
.date = .date("2026-10-28T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Oriel"],
.layout = "docs.shtml",
---

`oriel.video` shows a **native video player** over the app's window: the
video fills the window, with the platform's own controls (play, pause,
seek, volume), the title at the top and a close button. Closing it (that
button, Esc, Android's back) shows the page again, as it was.

A `<video>` element plays only what the WebView accepts. WebKitGTK refuses
MPEG-TS from camcorders (`.mts`, `.m2ts`), WMV/ASF and ProRes even when
GStreamer on the system plays them, and on some NVIDIA setups it can't
show video smoothly. The native player plays what the platform plays,
drawn by the GPU.

# [Availability]($heading.id('availability'))

| Platform | Player | URLs it takes |
|---|---|---|
| Linux | `GtkVideo` on a `GtkMediaFile` (GStreamer), graphics offload on GTK 4.14+ | `http(s)://` (the [media server]($link.page('docs/media-server'))'s), `file://` |
| Android | `MediaPlayer` on a `SurfaceView`, with a `MediaController` | `http(s)://`, `file://`, `content://`, the app's media URLs (`https://app.localhost/media/...`) |
| Windows, macOS, iOS | — (`available` is false) | |

The module is the `video` flag, **on by default** where it exists. With
the native renderer (`native_ui`) it is off: the player goes over a
WebView window. The page sees `window.oriel.features.video` and
`oriel.video.available`.

- **Linux** plays what the installed GStreamer plugins decode (`gst-libav`
  for most codecs, `gst-plugins-good` for http). On NVIDIA's driver an app
  built with the module uses GTK's GL renderer (`GSK_RENDERER=gl`, unless
  the user set one): drawing video frames, the default Vulkan renderer ran
  out of device memory and the app died in the driver. GtkVideo's graphics
  offload lets the compositor show frames without a copy, where the
  compositor and driver can.
- **Android** plays what `MediaPlayer` decodes (the device's codecs).
  Plain `http://` needs the app's network security config to allow
  cleartext to that host.
- **One player at a time.** `play` while one shows plays the new URL in
  it (no `video:closed` for the old one).
- **Embedded or covering.** By default the player covers the window; with
  `rect` it is embedded in the page ([Embedded in the page]($link.ref('embedded'))).

# [From the page]($heading.id('page'))

```js
if (oriel.video.available) {
  await oriel.video.play({ url, title: "Beach, 2019" }); // resolves once it shows
}
oriel.listen("video:ended", ({ url }) => { /* played to the end; still showing */ });
oriel.listen("video:error", ({ url, message }) => { /* the platform's message */ });
oriel.listen("video:audio-unsupported", ({ url }) => { /* Android: plays without sound */ });
oriel.listen("video:closed", ({ url }) => { /* the page shows again */ });
oriel.video.close(); // closes it from the page, if one shows
```

`play` rejects with `"Unsupported"` (not in this build or on this
platform), `"InvalidUrl"` (a scheme the player can't open), `"NoWindow"`
or `"Failed"` (Android: the app media path isn't there). A file that
can't be decoded, or a server that answers 404, shows the player and then
sends `video:error`: the player stays until it is closed, by the user or
by `oriel.video.close()`. On Android a video whose sound the device can't
decode (AC3 or DTS on most phones) still plays: the player says the sound
isn't supported, offers **Open in another app** (for URLs other apps can
open: not the app's media URLs), and sends `video:audio-unsupported`. The
player has a mute button. The events go to every window; each carries the
URL it is about.

`play` covers the window that called it, unless it is embedded. The
types are in the generated `oriel.ts` (`VideoApi`, `VideoPlayOptions`,
`VideoRect`, `VideoEvent`).

## [Embedded in the page]($heading.id('embedded'))

With `rect`, the player sits over that rectangle of the page (CSS pixels
from the viewport's top left), so the page's own bar, buttons and menus
stay around it. It shows no title and no close button of its own; it has
a **fullscreen** button.

```js
const slot = document.querySelector("#player-slot");
await oriel.video.play({ url, rect: slot.getBoundingClientRect() });
new ResizeObserver(() => oriel.video.setRect(slot.getBoundingClientRect())).observe(slot);
oriel.listen("video:fullscreen", ({ fullscreen }) => { /* its button, Esc or Back */ });
oriel.video.setFullscreen(true); // or the player's own button
```

- `setRect(rect)` moves it when the page's layout changes.
- `setFullscreen(on)` covers the screen: the window goes fullscreen
  (Linux) or the system bars hide and a landscape video turns the phone
  sideways (Android). Esc, Back or the button come back to the rectangle;
  `video:fullscreen` (`{ url, fullscreen }`) says so either way.
- Embedded and not fullscreen, Esc and Back are the page's (to close its
  viewer, say), and the page keeps the keyboard.
- Native views draw above the page: a menu or a dialog the page opens over
  the player would be hidden behind it. `setVisible(false)` hides the
  player while one shows and `setVisible(true)` brings it back in place
  (on Android the surface goes, so playback pauses and resumes).

# [From Zig]($heading.id('zig'))

```zig
if (oriel.video.available) {
    try oriel.video.play(null, .{ .url = url, .title = "Beach, 2019" });
}
oriel.video.close();
```

`play(window, options)`: `window` is an `*oriel.App.Window` (null: the
focused window, else `"main"`). It returns once the player shows, from any
thread (it waits for the main thread). The same events reach the page.

# [Examples]($heading.id('examples'))

## [Desktop: a media source over the media server]($heading.id('example-desktop'))

The [media server]($link.page('docs/media-server')) serves the app's bytes
with HTTP Range; GStreamer seeks with it. Here `library` is a
[`Source`]($link.page('docs/media-server').ref('source')).

```zig
try media.start(io, gpa, .{ .root_dir = "", .port = 17893, .source = library.source() });
```

```js
await oriel.video.play({ url: "http://127.0.0.1:17893/clips/00012.mts", title: "00012.MTS" });
```

A local file needs no server: `file:///home/me/Videos/00012.MTS`.

## [Android: the app's media URLs]($heading.id('example-android'))

`https://app.localhost/media/<path>` ([`media_scheme.base_url`]($link.page('docs/media-server'))) exists only inside the
WebView, which `MediaPlayer` can't ask. The player reads it in-process
instead, the way the WebView gets it: from the media source set with
`media_scheme.setSource` (its `readFn` is called on MediaPlayer's thread,
as the source contract allows), else from the file below the root set
with `media_scheme.setRoot`.

```zig
oriel.media_scheme.setSource(library.source()) catch |err|
    std.log.warn("media source: {s}", .{@errorName(err)});
```

```js
await oriel.video.play({ url: "https://app.localhost/media/clips/00012.mts", title: "00012.MTS" });
```

Other paths on the app's origin are refused (`"InvalidUrl"`): only its
media are files.

